1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,900
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:07,341
This is chapter nine: Optional and null strategy.

3
00:00:07,791 --> 00:00:10,431
Optional was added to solve one problem.

4
00:00:10,431 --> 00:00:15,907
A method that may have nothing to return should be able to say so in its signature.

5
00:00:15,907 --> 00:00:18,877
It was not added to eliminate null from Java.

6
00:00:18,877 --> 00:00:27,653
Treat it that way and you end up with Optional fields, Optional parameters and Optional lists, all worse than the null they replaced.

7
00:00:28,553 --> 00:00:34,807
In Java 7, a catalogue lookup that might find nothing just returns an InventoryItem.

8
00:00:34,807 --> 00:00:38,828
The signature is silent, so every caller has to guess.

9
00:00:38,828 --> 00:00:44,041
And the null checks nest deeper at every level: item, vendor, address.

10
00:00:44,491 --> 00:00:49,025
In Java 8, the return type says it: Optional of InventoryItem.

11
00:00:49,025 --> 00:00:51,657
And the caller becomes a flat chain.

12
00:00:51,657 --> 00:00:56,850
Map to the vendor, then the address, then the pincode, or else unknown.

13
00:00:56,850 --> 00:01:02,261
A missing SKU, vendor or address all end up at unknown, with no branching.

14
00:01:03,161 --> 00:01:10,521
Optional is a return type that says possibly nothing, plus a vocabulary for handling that without branching.

15
00:01:10,971 --> 00:01:15,345
If a value is present, map, flatMap and filter transform it.

16
00:01:15,795 --> 00:01:21,327
If it's empty, every operation just returns empty, without calling your function.

17
00:01:21,327 --> 00:01:25,288
That's what collapses the nested ifs into a flat pipeline.

18
00:01:25,738 --> 00:01:31,253
And at the end you must exit deliberately, with orElse, orElseGet or orElseThrow.

19
00:01:31,253 --> 00:01:33,091
There's no implicit unwrap.

20
00:01:33,091 --> 00:01:37,109
You choose what absence means, at the point where you know.

21
00:01:38,009 --> 00:01:39,746
Where does Optional belong?

22
00:01:39,746 --> 00:01:40,775
On return types.

23
00:01:40,775 --> 00:01:42,062
That's the use case.

24
00:01:42,512 --> 00:01:45,867
Not on fields: use null or a sensible default.

25
00:01:45,867 --> 00:01:48,275
Not on parameters: use overloads.

26
00:01:48,275 --> 00:01:51,703
Not inside collections: filter the empties out.

27
00:01:51,703 --> 00:01:57,319
And never Optional of a list, because then there are two ways to say nothing.

28
00:01:57,319 --> 00:01:59,216
Just return an empty list.

29
00:01:59,666 --> 00:02:02,081
The JDK itself is the guide.

30
00:02:02,081 --> 00:02:09,499
Optional appears as a return type on findFirst, min, max and reduce, and nowhere else.

31
00:02:09,499 --> 00:02:13,639
No JDK class has an Optional field or parameter.

32
00:02:14,539 --> 00:02:16,158
The API has two halves.

33
00:02:16,158 --> 00:02:22,562
To create one, use Optional dot of when null would be a bug, because it throws immediately.

34
00:02:22,562 --> 00:02:26,714
Use ofNullable at the boundary with code that returns null.

35
00:02:26,714 --> 00:02:31,007
Using ofNullable everywhere hides bugs that of would surface.

36
00:02:31,007 --> 00:02:36,918
Then stay inside the Optional with map, flatMap, filter, and Java 9's or and stream.

37
00:02:37,368 --> 00:02:39,529
To leave, choose deliberately.

38
00:02:39,529 --> 00:02:48,891
orElse with a default value, orElseGet with a lazy one, orElseThrow with your own exception, or ifPresentOrElse for both branches.

39
00:02:48,891 --> 00:02:49,900
And avoid get.

40
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:55,949
It throws on empty, exactly like the null dereference Optional was meant to prevent.

41
00:02:56,849 --> 00:02:59,232
Here's a cost hiding in plain sight.

42
00:02:59,232 --> 00:03:04,459
orElse takes a value, so its argument is evaluated before the call, every time.

43
00:03:04,459 --> 00:03:10,348
Here, the fallback price is computed on every lookup, even when the real price was found.

44
00:03:10,348 --> 00:03:14,120
With a side-effecting call, that's a bug, not just waste.

45
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:18,818
orElseGet takes a supplier, so it only runs when the Optional is empty.

46
00:03:18,818 --> 00:03:22,855
The same goes for exceptions: pass a supplier to orElseThrow.

47
00:03:23,305 --> 00:03:24,648
The rule is simple.

48
00:03:24,648 --> 00:03:29,385
orElse for literals and constants, orElseGet for anything computed.

49
00:03:30,285 --> 00:03:33,464
Composition follows the same rules as streams.

50
00:03:33,464 --> 00:03:36,505
Map when the function returns a plain value.

51
00:03:36,505 --> 00:03:42,864
FlatMap when it already returns an Optional, otherwise you'd get an Optional of an Optional.

52
00:03:42,864 --> 00:03:50,743
And filter turns a present but unsuitable value into empty, so a check joins the chain instead of interrupting it.

53
00:03:51,193 --> 00:04:00,637
To look up many things and keep the ones that exist, map each SKU to an Optional, then flatMap with Optional colon colon stream.

54
00:04:00,637 --> 00:04:03,588
Empties disappear, values are unwrapped.

55
00:04:04,038 --> 00:04:10,131
And or chains alternative sources: try the primary vendor, then the backup, then a default.

56
00:04:10,131 --> 00:04:15,153
Use or while you still want an Optional, and orElseGet when you're leaving.

57
00:04:16,053 --> 00:04:17,438
Four anti-patterns.

58
00:04:17,438 --> 00:04:19,261
One: Optional as a field.

59
00:04:19,261 --> 00:04:25,314
It allocates a wrapper per instance, and Optional isn't Serializable, deliberately.

60
00:04:25,314 --> 00:04:28,085
Null or a default says the same thing.

61
00:04:28,535 --> 00:04:30,422
Two: Optional as a parameter.

62
00:04:30,422 --> 00:04:34,198
The caller has to wrap, and you still have to handle null.

63
00:04:34,198 --> 00:04:41,228
If you see a caller writing Optional dot empty as an argument, it's doing work an overload should have done.

64
00:04:41,678 --> 00:04:43,531
Three: Optional of a list.

65
00:04:43,531 --> 00:04:46,880
Is nothing an empty Optional, or an empty list?

66
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:48,377
Return an empty list.

67
00:04:48,827 --> 00:04:51,045
Four: isPresent followed by get.

68
00:04:51,045 --> 00:04:55,691
That's the null check you were trying to avoid, plus an allocation.

69
00:04:55,691 --> 00:04:57,147
Use ifPresent or map.

70
00:04:58,047 --> 00:04:59,235
Three internals.

71
00:04:59,235 --> 00:05:01,613
Optional is a value-based class.

72
00:05:01,613 --> 00:05:10,676
Its identity is unspecified, so never compare two with double equals, never synchronise on one, and it isn't Serializable.

73
00:05:11,126 --> 00:05:13,257
Every Optional is an object.

74
00:05:13,257 --> 00:05:19,802
In a hot loop, the JIT's escape analysis often removes the allocation, but not always.

75
00:05:19,802 --> 00:05:27,488
In a measured hot path, returning a nullable value with a documented contract is a legitimate choice.

76
00:05:27,488 --> 00:05:29,771
Everywhere else, clarity wins.

77
00:05:30,221 --> 00:05:32,207
And get is a design regret.

78
00:05:32,207 --> 00:05:39,706
It's as unsafe as the null dereference it replaced, and its name makes it look like the normal way in.

79
00:05:39,706 --> 00:05:45,368
Java 10 added orElseThrow with no arguments as the honestly named equivalent.

80
00:05:46,268 --> 00:05:48,466
Optional covers return types.

81
00:05:48,466 --> 00:05:51,345
A real null strategy needs more tools.

82
00:05:51,795 --> 00:06:03,550
For parameters that must not be null, call Objects dot requireNonNull with the parameter name at the top of the method, so it fails at the boundary instead of fifteen frames later.

83
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,243
For collections, return an empty collection, never null.

84
00:06:07,693 --> 00:06:12,836
For fields, null plus a nullability annotation, or a default value.

85
00:06:12,836 --> 00:06:17,826
Pick one annotation library per codebase and use it consistently.

86
00:06:18,276 --> 00:06:24,655
When absence has behaviour, use a null object, like a vendor dot none with no-op methods.

87
00:06:25,105 --> 00:06:30,353
And at the boundary with older code that returns null, convert once with ofNullable.

88
00:06:31,253 --> 00:06:32,438
Production rules.

89
00:06:32,438 --> 00:06:37,944
Return Optional where absence is a normal outcome, like a lookup that may miss.

90
00:06:37,944 --> 00:06:41,777
Use of when null is a bug and ofNullable at boundaries.

91
00:06:41,777 --> 00:06:44,216
Chain with map, flatMap and filter.

92
00:06:44,216 --> 00:06:46,725
Use orElseGet for anything computed.

93
00:06:46,725 --> 00:06:52,998
When absence really is an error, orElseThrow with an exception that names the missing key.

94
00:06:52,998 --> 00:06:55,786
And requireNonNull on public parameters.

95
00:06:56,236 --> 00:06:59,426
Never use Optional for fields or parameters.

96
00:06:59,426 --> 00:07:04,719
Never wrap a collection in an Optional, or put Optionals in a collection.

97
00:07:04,719 --> 00:07:07,039
Never call get, use orElseThrow.

98
00:07:07,039 --> 00:07:09,286
Never write isPresent then get.

99
00:07:09,286 --> 00:07:12,404
Never compare Optionals with double equals.

100
00:07:12,404 --> 00:07:15,884
And don't wrap a value whose default is obvious.

101
00:07:16,784 --> 00:07:20,496
Java 8 introduced Optional and its primitive versions.

102
00:07:20,946 --> 00:07:27,005
Java 9 added stream, or, and ifPresentOrElse, which made composition much smoother.

103
00:07:27,455 --> 00:07:36,244
Java 10 added orElseThrow with no arguments, and Java 11 added isEmpty, which reads better than not isPresent.

104
00:07:36,694 --> 00:07:46,187
Java 14's helpful NullPointerException messages name the exact expression that was null, which narrowed the gap Optional was filling.

105
00:07:46,637 --> 00:07:57,176
And with records, sealed types and pattern matching, a sealed result like found or not found is now a good alternative when absence needs to carry a reason.

106
00:07:58,076 --> 00:07:58,707
To recap.

107
00:07:58,707 --> 00:08:03,407
Optional is for return types, so absence shows up in the signature.

108
00:08:03,407 --> 00:08:07,685
Chain it with map, flatMap and filter, and exit deliberately.

109
00:08:07,685 --> 00:08:11,613
orElse is eager, so use orElseGet for anything computed.

110
00:08:11,613 --> 00:08:15,541
Keep Optional out of fields, parameters and collections.

111
00:08:15,541 --> 00:08:21,713
And complete your null strategy with requireNonNull, empty collections and null objects.

112
00:08:22,163 --> 00:08:24,770
Next is chapter ten, java dot time.

113
00:08:24,770 --> 00:08:26,259
Thanks for watching.
